<section class="food-hero" aria-labelledby="food-title"><div class="food-hero-inner"><div class="food-hero-copy"><p class="eyebrow">EVERYDAY JAPAN · FOOD</p><h1 id="food-title">A little Japan<br>in every bite.</h1><p class="hero-lead">A rice ball for the ride. A warm bun between stops. Takoyaki fresh from the grill. Get to know six small foods for your Japan journey.</p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="#rice">Find your first bite <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><p class="hero-footnote">For train journeys, city walks and a break along the way.</p></div><div class="food-hero-pictures"><a class="hero-picture hero-picture-main" href="#onigiri"><img src="../images/foods/onigiri.webp" alt="Onigiri rice balls wrapped with dark nori seaweed" width="960" height="640" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><span>Onigiri <small>Rice, wrapped up.</small></span></a><a class="hero-picture" href="#nikuman"><img src="../images/foods/nikuman.webp" alt="A white steamed bun with a savoury pork filling" width="960" height="640" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><span>Nikuman <small>Warm &amp; soft.</small></span></a><a class="hero-picture" href="#takoyaki"><img src="../images/foods/takoyaki.webp" alt="Round takoyaki topped with sauce and bonito flakes" width="960" height="640" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><span>Takoyaki <small>A taste of Osaka.</small></span></a><p class="hero-picture-credit"><a href="#sources" data-open-sources="">Photo credits &amp; sources</a></p></div></div></section>

<div class="spot-page-shell"><aside data-spot-page-shared-module="rail"></aside><article class="spot-page-article food-article" id="food-content">
<div class="food-intro"><p class="food-hub-back"><a href="everyday-japan.html">← Everyday Japan</a></p><p class="eyebrow">SMALL FOODS, SMALL DISCOVERIES</p><h2>Start with something that catches your eye.</h2><p>At a station shop, a convenience store or a neighbourhood stall, an unfamiliar shape can be the start of a good story. Here are the names, flavours and small details that make a first bite easier.</p></div>
<nav class="food-chapters" aria-label="Food chapters"><a href="#rice"><span>01</span> Rice</a><a href="#warm"><span>02</span> Warm bites</a><a href="#sweet"><span>03</span> Sweet stops</a></nav>

<section class="food-section" id="rice" aria-labelledby="rice-title"><header class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">01 / RICE TO GO</p><h2 id="rice-title">A little parcel of rice.</h2><p>Two familiar shapes. Two very different bites.</p></header>
<article class="cs-spot food-card" id="onigiri"><figure class="cs-figure"><img src="../images/foods/onigiri.webp" alt="Japanese onigiri rice balls with nori seaweed" width="960" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><a href="#credit-onigiri" data-credit="onigiri">Ocdp · CC0</a></figcaption></figure><div class="cs-spot-body"><p class="food-kicker">RICE BALL · SAVOURY</p><h3>Onigiri</h3><p class="japanese-name" lang="ja">おにぎり</p><p>Rice shaped by hand, often with a filling and a wrap of <em>nori</em> seaweed. Triangle, round or something in between: there is more than one way to make an onigiri.</p><div class="filling-picker"><p class="picker-label" id="filling-label">What is inside? Choose a label to read.</p><div class="filling-buttons" role="group" aria-labelledby="filling-label"><button type="button" data-filling="salmon" aria-pressed="true"><span lang="ja">鮭</span> Salmon</button><button type="button" data-filling="tuna" aria-pressed="false"><span lang="ja">ツナマヨ</span> Tuna mayo</button><button type="button" data-filling="ume" aria-pressed="false"><span lang="ja">梅</span> Ume</button></div><p id="filling-description" aria-live="polite">Salmon: savoury flakes of fish tucked into the rice.</p><noscript><p>Tuna mayo: tuna with creamy mayonnaise. Ume: pickled plum with a tangy taste.</p></noscript></div><details class="wrapper-guide"><summary>How do I open the numbered wrapper?</summary><ol><li><b>1</b><span>Pull the top tab down and around the rice ball.</span></li><li><b>2</b><span>Gently slide the wrapper away from one side.</span></li><li><b>3</b><span>Slide the other side out, leaving the nori around the rice.</span></li></ol><p class="small-note">This is for the familiar three-part triangle wrapper. Follow the numbers and instructions on your own package; other wrappers open differently.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://www.u-tokyo.ac.jp/content/400098276.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">University of Tokyo guide · p. 29 ↗</a></details></div></article>
<article class="cs-spot food-card" id="inari"><figure class="cs-figure"><img src="../images/foods/inari.webp" alt="Inari sushi with rice tucked into golden fried-tofu pouches" width="960" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><a href="#credit-inari" data-credit="inari">jetalone · CC BY 2.0</a></figcaption></figure><div class="cs-spot-body"><p class="food-kicker">TOFU POUCH · SWEET &amp; SAVOURY</p><h3>Inari sushi</h3><p class="japanese-name" lang="ja">いなり寿司</p><p>Lightly vinegared rice tucked into a soft fried-tofu pouch simmered in a sweet soy sauce broth. The golden outside is tofu, rather than a pastry crust.</p><p class="taste-note"><strong>Look a little closer.</strong> You may find oblong or triangular shapes, with sesame seeds or vegetables mixed into the rice.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://www.kikkoman.com/en/culture/foodforum/recipe/traditional-34-1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Inside inari sushi · Kikkoman ↗</a></div></article>
</section>

<section class="food-section" id="warm" aria-labelledby="warm-title"><header class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">02 / WARM &amp; SAVOURY</p><h2 id="warm-title">Follow the steam.</h2><p>A soft bun or a little tray from the grill. Leave time to enjoy it.</p></header>
<aside class="cs-note-card warm-etiquette" aria-labelledby="etiquette-title"><h3 id="etiquette-title">A note before you board</h3><p>The smell of hot pork buns or takoyaki can bother nearby passengers in an enclosed carriage. To be considerate, enjoy these foods before boarding, in a place where eating is welcome.</p></aside>
<article class="cs-spot food-card" id="nikuman"><figure class="cs-figure"><img src="../images/foods/nikuman.webp" alt="Soft white steamed pork bun" width="960" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><a href="#credit-nikuman" data-credit="nikuman">鈴音雨 · CC BY 4.0</a></figcaption></figure><div class="cs-spot-body"><p class="food-kicker">STEAMED BUN · SOFT &amp; SAVOURY</p><h3>Nikuman / Butaman</h3><p class="japanese-name" lang="ja">肉まん / 豚まん</p><p>A soft steamed bun with a savoury pork filling. In Kansai, the region around Osaka and Kyoto, you will often see the name <em>butaman</em>.</p><p class="taste-note"><strong>The name is a clue.</strong> <span lang="ja">肉</span> means meat; <span lang="ja">豚</span> means pork. Vegetables and seasoning vary from shop to shop.</p><p>In Osaka, 551 HORAI is one name you may encounter: its pork buns pair diced pork and onion with a slightly sweet dough.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://osaka-info.jp/gourmet/gastronomy-butaman/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Osaka’s pork buns · Osaka tourism (Japanese) ↗</a><br><a class="source-link" href="https://www.551horai.co.jp/menu/butaman/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">551 HORAI pork buns (Japanese) ↗</a></div></article>
<article class="cs-spot food-card" id="takoyaki"><figure class="cs-figure"><img src="../images/foods/takoyaki.webp" alt="Takoyaki with sauce, mayonnaise and bonito flakes" width="960" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><a href="#credit-takoyaki" data-credit="takoyaki">sayo ts · CC0</a></figcaption></figure><div class="cs-spot-body"><p class="food-kicker">OCTOPUS BITES · OSAKA FAVOURITE</p><h3>Takoyaki</h3><p class="japanese-name" lang="ja">たこ焼き</p><p>Round bites of batter with octopus inside, cooked in a pan full of little hollows. Sauce, seaweed flakes and bonito flakes are familiar toppings; some versions add mayonnaise.</p><p class="taste-note"><strong>Take your time with the first bite.</strong> Freshly cooked takoyaki can be very hot inside. A small tray makes a lovely pause while exploring Osaka.</p><p class="small-note">Some shops make a crisp outer shell; others keep it softer. Both are part of the fun of trying a different shop.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://osaka-info.jp/gourmet/gastronomy-takoyaki/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Osaka’s takoyaki · Osaka tourism (Japanese) ↗</a></div></article>
</section>

<section class="food-section" id="sweet" aria-labelledby="sweet-title"><header class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">03 / A SWEET STOP</p><h2 id="sweet-title">Something sweet, before the next stop.</h2><p>A fish-shaped cake. A glossy skewer. Let the shape lead you in.</p></header>
<article class="cs-spot food-card" id="taiyaki"><figure class="cs-figure"><img src="../images/foods/taiyaki.webp" alt="Golden fish-shaped taiyaki cakes" width="960" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><a href="#credit-taiyaki" data-credit="taiyaki">SLPTWRK · CC BY 2.0</a></figcaption></figure><div class="cs-spot-body"><p class="food-kicker">FISH-SHAPED CAKE · SWEET FILLING</p><h3>Taiyaki</h3><p class="japanese-name" lang="ja">たい焼き</p><p>The fish is the shape. Inside this little cake, you will often find sweet red bean paste, called <em>anko</em>. Other fillings, such as custard, give you another reason to look at the menu.</p><p class="taste-note"><strong>A name to recognise.</strong> <span lang="ja">あんこ</span> is sweet bean paste. It appears in many Japanese sweets, so this can be the first of several discoveries.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://www.japan.travel/en/blog/japanese-desserts-to-try/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Japanese sweets · JNTO ↗</a></div></article>
<article class="cs-spot food-card" id="dango"><figure class="cs-figure"><img src="../images/foods/dango.webp" alt="Mitarashi dango skewers covered in a glossy soy sauce glaze" width="960" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><a href="#credit-dango" data-credit="dango">Ocdp · CC0</a></figcaption></figure><div class="cs-spot-body"><p class="food-kicker">RICE DUMPLINGS · SWEET SOY GLAZE</p><h3>Mitarashi dango</h3><p class="japanese-name" lang="ja">みたらし団子</p><p>Chewy rice dumplings on a skewer, coated in a glossy, sweet soy sauce glaze. The soy sauce adds a savoury note to the sweetness.</p><p class="taste-note"><strong>Look for the word.</strong> <span lang="ja">団子</span> means <em>dango</em>. The golden-brown glaze tells you this is the mitarashi kind; other dango wear different toppings.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://www.kikkoman.co.jp/homecook/washoku/097/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mitarashi dango · Kikkoman (Japanese) ↗</a></div></article>
</section>

<section class="food-section food-pause" aria-labelledby="pause-title"><div class="cs-note-card"><p class="eyebrow">A MOMENT FOR YOUR FOOD</p><h2 id="pause-title">For the ride, or a stop along the way.</h2><p>An onigiri is easy to bring along. For warm buns and takoyaki, make time for a pause near the shop before boarding: their aroma can be unwelcome to people sitting nearby. Choose a place where eating is welcome, and follow local signs.</p><p>Flavours, fillings and ingredients vary. Read the package or ask the shop about ingredients that matter to you.</p></div></section>
<section class="food-section food-next" aria-labelledby="next-title"><div class="food-pairing-heading"><p class="eyebrow">SOMETHING WITH YOUR BITE</p><h2 id="next-title">A bite. A sip. A little pause.</h2><p>Green tea with your onigiri, coffee with a sweet treat, or a Japanese beer with something savoury. Find a drink for your next food stop.</p></div><div class="food-pairings"><a class="everyday-story-card" href="drinks.html" data-cta-track="food_pairing_click" data-cta-id="foods_to_drinks"><span class="everyday-story-photo"><img src="../images/drinks/20261004-tea-shelf-michikusa.webp" alt="Bottled green and roasted teas on a Japanese shop shelf" width="1280" height="720" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></span><span class="everyday-story-copy"><small>TEA, COFFEE &amp; MORE</small><strong>Something to sip</strong><span>Get to know the flavours on the shelf.</span><b>Explore Japanese drinks <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></b></span></a><a class="everyday-story-card" href="beer-on-shinkansen.html" data-cta-track="food_pairing_click" data-cta-id="foods_to_beer"><span class="everyday-story-photo"><img src="../images/beer/20261004-beer-hero-michikusa.webp" alt="Japanese beer cans from several breweries on a shop shelf" width="1400" height="1220" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50% 35%"></span><span class="everyday-story-copy"><small>A BEER WITH YOUR FOOD</small><strong>Meet Japanese beer</strong><span>Familiar lagers and the breweries behind them.</span><b>Explore Japanese beer <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></b></span></a></div><p class="food-pairing-credit">Photographs: Michikusa.</p></section>

